<title>USA: U.S. March housing starts fell 6.4 percent.</title>
<headline>U.S. March housing starts fell 6.4 percent.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-04-16</dateline>
<text>
<p>Commerce housing starts and building permits, seasonally adjusted annual rates, in 1,000s:</p>
<p> PCT CHANGE: Mar     Feb (Prev)     Jan (Prev)   Mar97/96</p>
<p> -Starts    -6.4    10.7   12.2     1.6    0.7	 -0.3</p>
<p> -Permits   -1.5     3.1    3.0    -1.5   -1.5	 Unch</p>
<p> RATES     March     Feb (Prev)     Jan (Prev)   Mar 1996</p>
<p> -Starts   1,425   1,522  1,528   1,375  1,362	1,429</p>
<p> -Permits  1,423   1,444  1,442   1,400  1,400	1,423</p>
<p> STARTS:   March     Feb (Prev)     Jan (Prev)   Mar 1996</p>
<p> -Single   1,117   1,218  1,215   1,125  1,117	1,156</p>
<p> -Multiple   308     304    313     250    245	  273</p>
<p> PERMITS   March     Feb (Prev)     Jan (Prev)   Mar 1996</p>
<p> -Single   1,015   1,077  1,075   1,051  1,051	1,097</p>
<p> -Multiple   408     367    367     349    349	  326</p>
<p> MARCH STARTS--Pct--Rate   MARCH PERMITS-Pct---Rate</p>
<p> -NorthEast   11.5   146    -Northeast -10.5   145</p>
<p> -Midwest    -20.2   292    -Midwest    -2.2   309</p>
<p> -South	 -7.1   619    -South	 4.3   637</p>
<p> -West	   2.5   368    -West	 -6.5   332</p>
<p>Actual Starts and Permits, unadjusted in 1,000s:</p>
<p>    -    March   Feb   Jan  Mar96</p>
<p> Starts    115    93    82   116</p>
<p> Permits   118    93    89   120</p>
<p>Wall Street economists had forecast March starts at a 1.46 million unit annual rate.</p>
<p>March starts of single-family homes were down 8.3 percent from February, and multiple-unit starts up 1.3 percent. March permits for single-family units down 5.8 percent, while permits for multiple unit dwelling were up 11.2 percent.  </p>
<p>The department said the 20.2 percent drop in housing starts in the Midwest in March was the largest since January 1994, when they were down 25.8 percent.</p>
